{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_014VcEKspdYWq4vRU6ySiFTP","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Wait, the fallback path for logup is broken - I'm trying to call `evaluate_round0_interactions_gpu` but that requires the symbolic constraints and other data that I don't have easily accessible in the fallback. The logup batch metadata already has the per-AIR data (rules, weights) on the GPU, but the fallback requires calling the full function again which would redo the CPU work.\n\nActually, the problem is that the fallback for logup is much more complex because the per-AIR CPU work (DAG building, weight computation) was already done and the data was uploaded to device. \n\nI can't easily call `evaluate_round0_interactions_gpu` again because that would redo the CPU work and upload. Let me simplify: instead of the complex fallback, just avoid batching when the budget would be exceeded. I can check the budget BEFORE collecting metadata in the first loop. If skip_domain * max_num_cosets_possible * expected_total_blocks * sizeof(T) would exceed the budget, simply don't batch at all. But we don't know the exact sizes until we iterate, so let me try a different approach.\n\nThe real memory pressure comes from the temporary buffers—they're large because they accumulate per-block partial sums. I already drop them right after each kernel completes, but the output buffers stay alive until the D2H transfer in Phase 2. So the peak memory during the zerocheck batch is the temp buffer plus the output buffer plus metadata, then after that drops I have the logup temp and output buffers, and finally just the output buffers remain.\n\nLooking at the actual numbers, the logup batch is where memory peaks: around 466 MB total with the current tmp buffer sizes. But I'm wondering if I should just use the actual max_temp_bytes from the prover config to make the batching decision more robust. Let me first verify whether the original code even hits this OOM issue or if it's something my changes introduced.\n\nIf I disable batching entirely by setting the budget to zero, does the benchmark still pass? That would tell me whether batching is actually the culprit. Rather than trying to recover from budget overruns by re-running individual launches, I should prevent those AIRs from being batched in the first place. The simplest fix is a pre-scan that estimates total batch memory and disables batching if it exceeds the budget. Even better, I can just check the skip_domain size upfront since that's the main memory driver. Actually, the cleanest approach is to stop using a separate tmp buffer and reuse the existing per-AIR buffers instead.\n\nFor the fallback, I'll just set a no_batch flag before the per-AIR loop if the budget check fails, forcing everything through the individual path. I could also use skip_domain as a heuristic—if it's small enough (≤32), batching is safe; if it's large, individual launches already saturate the GPU anyway. Though I need to recalculate: with skip_domain = 1024, num_x can be at most 31 since num_x * 1024 must stay under 32768.\n\nActually, the real insight is that batching helps most when both skip_domain and num_x are small, creating kernels with only a few hundred threads. With large skip_domain, individual launches already have plenty of GPU utilization, so batching adds memory overhead without much benefit. The simplest fix is to just check if the temporary memory fits the budget before allocating—if not, skip batching entirely and rerun with it disabled. I'll estimate the memory requirements upfront by checking the skip_domain and constraint_degree values, then decide whether to disable batching before entering the per-AIR loop.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5046,"cache_read_input_tokens":164145,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemer