mit at around 478 KB, so batching works fine. But at skip value 10, the constraint degree multiplied by the skip domain balloons to 3072, which pushes the temporary buffer estimate to 490 MB—exceeding the budget and forcing the system to fall back to individual kernel launches. This is frustrating because the profiling data shows Round 0 is spending 570ms on what appears to be many sequential small launches, which suggests batching could help significantly. The real constraint here is that the memory budget only permits batching when the skip value stays small enough, so my optimization can't actually improve performance on this benchmark if the skip parameter is set too high.\n\nI think the solution is to implement sub-batching—processing the AIRs in smaller chunks of maybe 50 at a time rather than all at once. This way I can keep the temporary buffer footprint reasonable while still reducing the total number of kernel launches from 500+ down to around 10. Let me first verify what the actual skip value is for this benchmark by checking the MLE rounds count in the profiling data.\n\nActually, I should just increase the memory budget to 512 MB since the RTX 4090 has plenty of capacity and the temporary buffer gets freed after each kernel anyway. The real issue seems to be that the VPMM pool isn't releasing memory properly even after I explicitly drop the buffer, which is causing the second segment to fail. But wait—the original code without any batching also fails on the second segment, so maybe this isn't actually a batching problem at all.\n\nLet me think about this differently: when batching is disabled due to the budget check, my code falls back to the exact same per-AIR kernel launches as the original, so the memory behavior should be identical. The 6.5 GB peak I'm seeing matches what happens without my changes, which means the OOM on segment 2 is a pre-existing issue unrelated to my optimization.\n\nI should set the budget to something reasonable like 256 MB to see if batching actually activates based on the real l_skip value, and whether the benchmark can complete. But first, I need to figure out what l_skip actually is—let me add a temporary log statement to see the actual values.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":397,"cache_read_input_tokens":188500,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":397},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"297e37a8-7de7-4f8d-9cb1-f45048fd2150","uuid":"adc908bf-9acc-42d2-a57e-06de0ac29ecc"}
